{\xe \v aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON\:mesh.h}
{\xe \v mesh.h\:aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON}
{\qr aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACEV}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACEV}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
A higher-level polygon with more than 3 edges.\par
A triangle is a polygon, but polygon in this context means "all polygons that are not triangles". The "Triangulate"-Step is provided for your convenience, it splits all polygons in triangles (which are much easier to handle). \par
}\cell }{\row }
{\xe \v aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag\:mesh.h}
{\xe \v mesh.h\:aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag}
{\qr aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag{\bkmkstart AAAAAAACEW}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAACEW}
\cell }{{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
A flag to determine whether this triangles only mesh is NGON encoded.\par
NGON encoding is a special encoding that tells whether 2 or more consecutive triangles should be considered as a triangle fan. This is identified by looking at the first vertex index. 2 consecutive triangles with the same 1st vertex index are part of the same NGON.\par
At the moment, only quads (concave or convex) are supported, meaning that polygons are 'seen' as triangles, as usual after a triangulation pass.\par
To get an NGON encoded mesh, please use the aiProcess_Triangulate post process.\par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
